In this lesson, we'll look at a really handy tool called a two-way table, which lets you lay out two categorical variables in a simple grid. Picture a little box with two rows and two columns, and the four cells inside cover every possible combination of outcomes for your two events. The top left cell is where both events happen together. The top right is where the first event happens but the second one doesn't. The bottom left is where the first event doesn't happen but the second one does. And the bottom right is where neither event happens. These four cells cover everything, so their probabilities always add up to one. From those four cells you can read off almost anything you need. To find the probability of just the first event, you add the two cells in its row. To find the second event, you add the two cells in its column. The joint cell gives you both events together, and the bottom right corner gives you neither. One special case to watch for is when two events are mutually exclusive, meaning they can't both happen, so that joint cell is empty. Now let's see it in action.